Unify Desktop and Android Accounts with Real-Time Secure Chat Synchronization

The QGuard Messaging Desktop and QGuard Messaging Android applications must function as one unified ecosystem.

A user should only need one QGuard Helix account to access both applications.

Primary Objective

Implement a unified authentication and synchronization architecture so that:

Desktop and Android use the same login credentials.
Users sign in with the same email and password.
The same account is recognized across all devices.
Secure Chat conversations synchronize in real time between Desktop and Android.
Subscription status is shared across both applications.

The experience should be similar to applications such as Signal, WhatsApp Multi-Device, or Telegram, where users can seamlessly continue conversations across multiple devices.

Authentication

Implement a single authentication system.

A user should:

Register once.
Verify their email once.
Log in using the same credentials on Desktop and Android.
Have one user profile shared across all devices.

The account should include:

User ID
Email
Display Name
Profile Photo
Subscription Plan
Device List
Public Encryption Keys
Identity Keys
Security Settings
Subscription Synchronization

Subscription information must be shared across devices.

For example:

If a user purchases the STARTER subscription on Desktop:

Android should immediately recognize the active subscription.
The user should not need to purchase another subscription.
Premium features should automatically unlock on both Desktop and Android.

Subscription data should be tied to the user's account, not to a specific device.

Secure Chat Synchronization

Secure Chat should synchronize in real time between Desktop and Android.

When a user:

Sends a message from Desktop

the message should instantly appear on Android.

Likewise:

Sending from Android should immediately appear on Desktop.

Synchronization should include:

New messages
Edited messages (if supported)
Deleted messages (if supported)
Read receipts
Delivery status
Typing indicators
Attachments
Media
Conversation list
Last message preview
Unread counts

All synchronization must occur in real time.

Multi-Device Support

A user may be signed into multiple devices simultaneously.

Examples:

Windows Desktop
Android Phone
Future iPhone
Future macOS
Future Linux

All devices should remain synchronized.

End-to-End Encryption

Maintain end-to-end encryption across all devices.

Each device should have:

Its own identity key pair.
Its own session keys.
Its own prekeys.
Its own device identifier.

Messages should be encrypted for every authorized device associated with the user's account, ensuring that all registered devices can decrypt messages securely.

Quantum Vault

The Quantum Vault should NOT synchronize automatically between devices.

Each device maintains its own independent encrypted vault.

Example:

Desktop Quantum Vault

Encrypt local folders
Encrypt local files
Encrypt local drives

Android Quantum Vault

Encrypt phone storage
Encrypt downloads
Encrypt documents
Encrypt photos

These vaults remain device-specific and should not automatically share encrypted content.

Recovery Keys (Recommended Design)

The Recovery Key should be account-based, not device-based.

A single Recovery Key can securely protect access to the user's Quantum Vaults across all registered devices, while the encrypted vault contents remain stored locally on each device.

Example:

User Account:

john@example.com

Devices:

Windows PC
Android Phone

Recovery Key:

One Recovery Key associated with the account

Vault Contents:

Windows vault contains Windows-specific encrypted files.
Android vault contains Android-specific encrypted files.

The Recovery Key enables recovery of the encryption keys on authorized devices but does not synchronize the encrypted files themselves.

Why this design?

It provides:

One Recovery Key to remember.
Simplified disaster recovery.
Consistent account experience.
Reduced user confusion.
Device independence.
Better scalability for future platforms (macOS, Linux, iOS).

If a user loses their Android phone, the Windows vault remains unaffected. Likewise, changes to files in one vault do not automatically affect the other.

Device Registration

Implement a trusted device management system.

Each device should have:

Unique Device ID
Device Name
Platform
Registration Date
Last Active Timestamp
Device Public Key
Device Status (Trusted, Revoked, Pending)

Users should be able to view and manage all authorized devices from their account.

